plan Runtime
title   Applications, readiness, actors and context
status  proposed
goal
    One Runtime runs every host, React tree, server request, Story and CLI command: a closed set
    of machines, one store, one ordered decision path per actor, causally complete context and
    truthful cleanup. Turns are @plan.Machines.O3, operations @plan.Operations.O1, storage
    @plan.Persistence.O1, React @plan.React.O1, hydration @plan.ServerRendering.O1 and lazy
    entries @plan.LazyFeatures.O1.
outcomes
    O1  Apps
        app({ id, machines }) is inert and lists every machine the Runtime may run. An entry is a
        Machine, { machine, context? }, or a lazy { id, load, context? } (@plan.LazyFeatures.O1).
        The list is closed for the Runtime's lifetime: no runtime registration or child actors.
        Construction throws FlowError on unknown fields, duplicate machine ids, one machine under
        two keys, and colliding operation, domain or entity identities; lazy entries are checked the
        same way when adopted, before any work. Constructing an app, setup or ref runs no callback,
        loader, service, storage access, actor, timer or evidence.

        A machine entry's context is the default wiring for every actor of that machine; a shared
        ref may override declared slots. The resolved wiring must cover every declared slot with a
        compatible provider. Call sites (ensure, create, hooks) never supply wiring. Durable
        identity is app id, machine id and actor id, never an app property key.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/app.ts.
    O2  Setup and readiness
        runtimeSetup({ app, services?, scope?, persistence?, repairSlots?, audit? }).construct() is
        synchronous and inert. scope is an optional nonempty string naming whose data the Runtime
        holds, such as a user, workspace or tenant id. It partitions saved data
        (@plan.Persistence.O1) and stamps and checks hydration payloads (@plan.ServerRendering.O6);
        machines, operations and conditions never read it. repairSlots (default 6) and audit
        (default false, for development and tests only) are @plan.EntityDomains.O5 and
        @plan.EntityDomains.O4. services is an Effect Layer that must provide every service the
        app's operation adapters need, lazy entries included; a missing service is a type error.
        Each service is acquired once per Runtime.

        ready() is the one readiness Effect; FlowProvider calls it in browsers (@plan.React.O1). The
        first call starts the bootstrap; concurrent and later callers share its result, including a
        failure (no implicit retry). Interrupting one waiter interrupts only that wait. Bootstrap
        order:

        1. With persistence, claim scope ownership (@plan.Persistence.O7).
        2. Acquire storage, then read and validate persisted data before any app service starts
        (@plan.Persistence.O5).
        3. Merge restored data with any hydrated items (@plan.ServerRendering.O5).
        4. Acquire the remaining services, install restored and hydrated actors (providers before
        consumers), establish silent context baselines, then open the Runtime to callers.
        Transferred lazy entries load after ready resolves (@plan.LazyFeatures.O4).

        A service acquisition failure fails ready() with FlowError and its Cause, releases what was
        acquired in reverse order, and leaves no partial actor or evidence. Before ready succeeds,
        ensureActor and createActor reject with RuntimeNotReady; React's pre-attach handles
        (@plan.React.O2) are the only exception. Disposing during bootstrap cancels it and closes
        admission; a service acquired late is released and no handle is ever exposed.
        runtime.runPromise runs an Effect with the installed services; there is no runPromiseExit.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/setup.ts, app/host.ts, app/persistence.ts.
    O3  Actor identity
        actorRef(machine, id, { persist?, retain?, context? }) is inert. id is a string or a
        readonly array of strings; arrays encode unambiguously, so ["a/b", "c"] and ["a", "b/c"] are
        different actors. Two refs with the same machine and id but different persist, retain or
        context reject with ConflictingActorRef and change nothing.

        runtime.ensureActor(ref, { input }) returns the one shared actor for that identity, creating
        or restoring it. The first accepted input initializes it; later ensures never reset memory,
        and a different later input reports InputIgnored. A failed admission leaves nothing behind,
        so a later ensure may retry with new input. An input-free machine is ensured with no
        options. runtime.createActor(machine, { input }) always creates a new local actor that
        cannot persist or be a send target. Handles expose send, getSnapshot and snapshots.

        Disposing and then ensuring the same ref creates a new incarnation. Ensure while disposing
        returns at once a handle that reads the pre-attach snapshot and binds the new incarnation
        after cleanup. No outcome, schedule, issue or send from an old incarnation ever reaches a
        new one.

        A use is an attached React consumer, a live snapshots subscription or a bound context
        consumer. With retain (a Duration), a shared actor is disposed once retain has passed since
        its last use ended; a new use cancels that. A provider with bound consumers never expires.
        Without retain a shared actor lives until explicit or Runtime disposal. Retain expiry of a
        persisted actor disposes the live actor but keeps its saved record, so a later ensure
        restores it; only explicit disposeActor or forget removes saved state.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.ref.ts, app/host.ts.
    O4  Mailboxes and send
        Each actor processes events one at a time in FIFO order; a send during a turn is queued,
        never run inside the current turn. Each actor queues at most 1,024 pending events: an
        external send beyond that throws AdmissionOverloaded and changes nothing. send validates the
        event's tag and payload; an internal, unknown or invalid event throws InvalidEvent and
        starts no turn. Sends to a disposing or disposed actor are dropped and reported as
        SendTargetAbsent, never thrown. Outcomes, schedule firings and context updates are never
        dropped: they are bounded by work already admitted, not by the mailbox limit. Stream values
        coalesce (@plan.Operations.O4). Facts from an ended activation are discarded by design
        (@plan.Machines.O5).

        React's pre-attach handles and their send buffer are @plan.React.O2 and @plan.React.O3.


        An action send(ref, event) delivers a public event to a shared actor after the sender's turn
        commits, as a later turn of the target. If the target is absent or disposing the event is
        dropped with SendTargetAbsent; if it is full, AdmissionOverloaded. Both are reported on the
        sender and never roll back its turn. Several sends keep their order.

        Every publication is one complete cut: a reader never sees an actor's new snapshot with old
        store data or the reverse. A new snapshots subscriber gets current truth first; slow
        subscribers may skip intermediate snapshots. Snapshots expose no revision numbers or raw
        Cause; each belongs to one store revision, which passive reads of it use. A snapshots
        subscription completes only at the actor's disposal, after its terminal snapshot.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts.
    O5  Context propagation
        Provider.select(selector) reads one complete provider snapshot. A context slot binds to one
        provider for the consumer's lifetime. Context reads form an acyclic upward graph; a cycle,
        missing or incompatible provider rejects at admission. Baselines are silent: onContext fires
        for none established at admission, restore, lazy adoption or hydration. A context change
        enqueues the consumer's declared event; it never runs a transition, memory patch or action
        directly, and never resets memory. Memory that belongs to one user, workspace or account is
        keyed into the actor id, or lives in a Runtime per scope (@O7).

        When a provider changes, each affected consumer receives at most one context update per
        wave, computed from the latest complete provider snapshots, and never a mix of new and stale
        values from the same cause (diamonds included). Slots compare with Equal.equals; an equal
        value causes no update, and consumers waiting on that branch continue. A context update
        keeps its place in the FIFO queue; later events never overtake it.

        A consumer selector that throws keeps that consumer's last valid context, publishes one
        issue, and retries only on a newer provider snapshot; the provider and other consumers are
        unaffected. Persistence capture never observes a half-propagated wave; it waits for the wave
        to settle (@plan.Persistence.O3).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/queue/queue.machine.ts.
    O6  Lifecycle and disposal
        Public lifecycle is active, disposing or disposed. runtime.disposeActor(actor) disposes the
        actor for every user. Disposing a provider while consumers are bound rejects with
        BlockedByDependents naming them. Actor and Runtime disposal are idempotent: repeated calls
        share one result. Once Runtime disposal starts, ready, ensureActor, createActor and
        runPromise reject with RuntimeDisposed. Runtime disposal closes admission first, then
        disposes consumers before providers; once a provider starts closing, no consumer can bind to
        it.

        On disposal, queued events that have not started are dropped without running. Schedules
        cancel, activities stop, and invoked commits are allowed to settle. Runtime disposal is
        bounded: after 5 seconds a commit still in flight becomes unknown with its Attempt, and its
        late result is ignored. A cleanup failure rejects dispose with CleanupFailed, quarantines
        the ref (later ensures report the same failure) and keeps the full Cause; it is never
        reported as success. Runtime disposal waits for the final persistence write within the same
        5-second budget; a write still pending then reports StorageFailure (@plan.Persistence.O3).
        The terminal snapshot keeps the last committed state and memory; disposal runs no machine
        event.

        runtime.dispose({ forget: true }) also deletes the scope's stored records
        (@plan.Persistence.O5); plain dispose keeps them.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/host.ts.
    O7  Isolation
        Runtimes share no mutable state: actors, store, services, storage, clock or evidence.
        Handles from one Runtime are rejected by another; nested React providers use the nearest
        Runtime. A different user or account is a new Runtime with a different scope
        (@plan.React.O1). Every server request and Story run constructs its own Runtime; a
        module-level server Runtime is never used. Story storage is always fresh memory storage
        (@plan.Testing.O3).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/next/providers.tsx.
decisions
    D1  The app is a closed list of machines
        Lazy entries name machines the app already declares; nothing registers at runtime, so the
        structural representation stays complete for tools.
    D2  Wiring lives on refs and app defaults, never at call sites
        One place declares where an actor's context comes from, so tools can draw the graph.
constraints
    C1  One execution path
        Hosts, requests, React, Stories and the CLI use the same Runtime; fixtures replace services,
        never the kernel.
    C2  Identities are never reused
        Actor incarnations, request generations and Attempt ids never wrap or repeat within a
        Runtime or across restore; exhausting a counter refuses new work with IdentityExhausted
        before any change.
verification
    V1  Construction: closed-list errors, unambiguous id parts and inert constructors.
    V2  Services: a missing adapter service is a type error; each service is acquired once.
    V3  Readiness: storage before validation, shared failure, interrupted waiter, second-tab
        refusal, no partial handles after failure.
    V4  Identity: first input wins with a diagnostic, conflicting refs reject, re-ensure after
        dispose ignores every late result of the old incarnation, retain expiry on the TestClock.
    V5  Mailboxes: 1,024 and 64 bounds, invalid events, FIFO, send failures owned by the sender.
    V6  Context: diamond and chain waves give one update from latest values; a throwing selector is
        isolated.
    V7  Disposal and isolation: provider-with-consumers rejection, idempotent dispose, 5-second
        bound with unknown commits, quarantine on cleanup failure, no sharing between Runtimes.
questions
    Q1  Can one services Layer be acquired storage-first?
        Recommended: prove it against Effect 4.0.5 in S1; if not, acquire the whole Layer before
        restore validation.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Id byte encoding; counter headroom sizes; evidence ordering details; lifecycle evidence
        causes; the private shutdown budget split.
